---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MoonPhase from '@/components/tools/calc2/MoonPhase.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="moon-phase-calculator">
  <MoonPhase client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the moon phase calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The moon phase calculator finds the new moon before your date and the next new moon, first quarter,
      full moon and last quarter. It computes each phase time from the moon's mean motion with the
      periodic corrections in Meeus, Astronomical Algorithms, chapter 49, which agree with published
      times to about a minute. The lit part of the disc comes from the phase angle i between the sun and
      the earth as seen from the moon.
    </p>
    <pre><code>age          = date − last new moon            (0 to 29.53 days)
phase angle  i ≈ 180° − D − 6.289° sin M′ + 2.100° sin M − 1.274° sin(2D − M′) − …
lit fraction k = (1 + cos i) ÷ 2
D: mean elongation of the moon, M: sun's mean anomaly, M′: moon's mean anomaly</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      On 8 April 2024 the new moon was at 18:21 UTC, during the total solar eclipse over North America.
      The calculator gives 18:21 UTC. Fourteen days later the moon is waxing gibbous, about 99% lit, and
      it is full on 23 April at 23:49 UTC.
    </p>

    <h2>Phase names</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>New moon, first quarter, full moon and last quarter are moments; the calculator uses the name for the day around them.</li>
      <li>Between them the moon is a crescent (less than half lit) or gibbous (more than half lit), waxing before the full moon and waning after it.</li>
      <li>First quarter is half lit: the moon has gone a quarter of its orbit since the new moon.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The lit percentage uses the main terms of the moon's motion and is within about 1 percentage point.
      Phase times are for the whole earth; the date of a phase depends on your time zone. Times are shown
      in your device's time zone.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
